{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "earthseed",
  "compatibility_date": "2025-01-01",
  "main": "src/worker/index.ts",
  // Two schedules, on clocks that differ by three orders of magnitude. The handler branches on
  // `event.cron`, so the strings here are load-bearing — change one and change it there too.
  //
  //   "* * * * *"  close viewing sessions whose heartbeat stopped (SESSION_STALE_SECONDS = 150).
  //                Measured in seconds, so it needs a minute tick. Note that the LIVE viewer
  //                count does not depend on this cron at all — it is computed from the heartbeat
  //                watermark and stays correct if the cron never fires. What needs it is the
  //                recorded DURATION of a session that ended without a pagehide.
  //
  //   "0 * * * *"  null reported still frames once their retention window passes
  //                (REPORT_FRAME_RETENTION_DAYS, default 30). Hourly rather than daily because
  //                the window is measured in days — an hour of slop either side is free, and an
  //                hourly tick is easier to confirm is alive than one that fires at 04:00.
  //                Reports under a preservation hold are exempt in SQL, not here; see 0010.
  "triggers": { "crons": ["* * * * *", "0 * * * *"] },
  // ── RELAY BACKEND: moq.pro (Mode A) since 20 Sep 2026 ────────────────────────────────────────
  //
  // Everything in this section about the tinymoq broker is now the DORMANT alternative. The
  // MOQ_PRO_JWK secret is set, so moqProAssign() answers first and every broadcast goes through
  // cdn.moq.pro with a per-broadcast token this Worker mints. earthseed.live now shares a CDN
  // with vivoh.earth and wallflower.tv.
  //
  // THE VARS BELOW PROVE NOTHING ABOUT WHERE TRAFFIC GOES. They stay populated on purpose, so
  // that moving between backends is a secret change rather than a deploy — and so the rollback
  // is `wrangler secret delete MOQ_PRO_JWK`, with no code to revert. The only honest answer to
  // "which CDN is this on" is `npx wrangler secret list`.
  //
  // The signing key is Earthseed's OWN (EdDSA, kid f-UtSYfdV_QdZntX2QzEqzaRHlCBOgC3an9gRQUHA48,
  // generated 20 Sep 2026). Deliberately not vivoh.earth's or wallflower.tv's: one key across
  // products means a compromise of any one Worker forges tokens for all of them. moq.pro holds
  // only the public half, so it can verify our tokens and cannot mint one.
  //
  // Its PUBLIC half is committed as ./earthseed-moqpro.public.jwk, and that file earns its place:
  // Cloudflare secrets are write-only, so once the private half is deployed NOTHING otherwise
  // records which key is live. That absence is what once turned a one-line misconfiguration into
  // an afternoon. (./earthseed-ed25519.public.jwk is the different, older key — the tinymoq fleet
  // BYOK one, kid X3Kz…. Two keys, two backends; do not paste one where the other belongs.)
  //
  // MOQ_PRO_ROOT is unset, so the namespace is "erik" — shared with the other two products. For
  // them that is an accepted collision risk. Here it is not a risk at all: an earthseed broadcast
  // name is a 52-character base32 Ed25519 PUBLIC KEY, so a collision is a key collision, and
  // taking someone's name would mean holding their private half.
  //
  // What did NOT change: the content key is still derived in the two browsers from the `#k=`
  // fragment, which browsers never transmit. cdn.moq.pro carries ciphertext it cannot read,
  // exactly as the fleet did. Moving CDN moves who fans out the bytes, not who can decode them.
  //
  // ── The dormant path ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // STREAMING FLEET: the tinymoq broker (cdnadmin) and our own relay boxes.
  //
  // The Worker now sits in front of the broker: it POSTs {broadcast, role, …} to
  // <BROKER_BASE>/cdn/assign with the CDN_API_TOKEN secret. That token replaced the public `pk_`
  // that used to ship in the page, which is the change that makes admission and the kill switch
  // mean anything — a credential everyone can read cannot be declined.
  //
  // Media always goes browser <-> relay box. Neither the broker nor this Worker ever carries it,
  // and neither can decrypt it.
  //
  // ROLLBACK if the Worker path misbehaves: there is no one-line var for it. The client reaches
  // /api/broadcast/start and /api/watch/start on its own origin; going back to direct-to-broker
  // means reverting simple/earthseed.js. That is intentional — a config flag that silently
  // bypassed admission would be a hole with a switch on it.
  "vars": {
    // ── THE BROADCAST SHUTTER ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // "1" while the relay fleet is shut down: /api/broadcast/{start,challenge} answer 503 and the
    // client shows a modal instead of walking someone into a broker that is not there.
    //
    // STILL "1" AS OF 20 Sep 2026, and the reason it closed has now gone away: this deployment
    // is on moq.pro, which is up, rather than on the fleet that was down. What is NOT yet done is
    // registering the public half of MOQ_PRO_JWK with moq.pro's "Import Asymmetric". Until that
    // is in place the CDN does not know the key — and an unregistered key does not refuse the
    // connection, it opens a WebTransport session that dies the moment it speaks MoQ. Reopening
    // before then would produce a broadcast that looks like it started and carries nothing.
    //
    // So: register the key, THEN set this to "0" and deploy.
    //
    // TO REOPEN: set this to "0" and `npm run deploy`. That is the whole procedure — the client
    // reads it from /api/config and needs no edit. Bring the relays up FIRST; this var only
    // controls what we say, not whether anything is actually listening.
    //
    // Only "1" and "true" close it. Anything else, including unset, is open — a fat-fingered var
    // must not take broadcasting down, and "0"/"false" are the values someone reaches for to turn
    // it off (both of which are truthy strings, which is the bug this avoids).
    "BROADCAST_OFFLINE": "1",
    "OFFLINE_MESSAGE": "Temporarily offline. Please contact erik@vivoh.com for a demo.",

    "BROKER_BASE": "https://tinymoq.com",
    // Kept so a rollback to the pre-Worker arrangement needs no code change; BROKER_BASE wins.
    "FLEET_MODE": "brokered",
    "FLEET_ENDPOINT": "https://tinymoq.com/cdn/assign",
    // The publishable key the client used to carry. Read ONLY as a fallback when CDN_API_TOKEN is
    // unset, so a misconfigured deploy degrades to the old behaviour instead of refusing everyone.
    "PUBLIC_KEY": "pk_Am-UpPEuGCt5dsnR8xqzOFX2mEYDSCeMGrDWxXli4LU",
    // Who mints the relay token. "worker" = we sign it with MOQ_AUTH_PRIVATE_JWK and therefore
    // choose its lifetime, which is what makes a kill bind a client that ignores the stop signal.
    // "broker" = pass the broker's token through, and the expiry guarantee is whatever it chose.
    //
    // TRIED AND REVERTED, 14 Aug 2026 — do not flip this back without doing the fleet work first.
    // Measured: with the publishable key the broker mints a usable token signed with ITS kid
    // (iZ-AkZzB…), valid for 24 hours. Minting here instead, with our own kid (X3KzNJpR…, the one
    // GET /api/pubkey publishes), produced a token the box REFUSED — the WebTransport connect to
    // dal.moqcdn.net:8007 failed with QUIC_IETF_GQUIC_ERROR_MISSING and the WebSocket fallback
    // timed out. So our verify_jwk is registered with the broker but is not what the relay boxes
    // check, at least not these ones.
    //
    // This matters, and it is the one place where Earthseed is weaker than Wallflower today:
    // 24 hours is how long a terminated stream stays watchable by a client that ignores the stop
    // signal, and VIEWER_TOKEN_TTL below cannot shorten a token we did not sign. Cooperating
    // clients still stop in ~5 seconds, and nobody new is placed at all.
    //
    // TO FIX: register kid X3KzNJpRvVarbKBM2mk_M5JGt0dDYu85ZA5z2nLb1Qk as a verify_jwk on the
    // relay boxes themselves (relay.toml [auth]), then set this to "worker", drop
    // VIEWER_TOKEN_TTL to ~120, and re-run scripts/e2e/broadcast-watch.mjs — it fails loudly if
    // the token is refused, which is how this was caught.
    "TOKEN_SOURCE": "broker",
    // Relay token lifetimes, seconds. The viewer figure is the ceiling on how long a terminated
    // stream can still be watched by a client that ignores the stop signal; lower is stricter and
    // costs a reconnect that often. 3600 while TOKEN_SOURCE is "broker" (where it does nothing).
    "PUBLISHER_TOKEN_TTL": "43200",
    "VIEWER_TOKEN_TTL": "3600",
    // Publish codes. Config, not code — tuning any of these is a `wrangler deploy`, not an edit.
    //   PUBLISH_CODE_DELAY_HOURS is the one that matters. At 0 a code works the moment it is
    //   issued, which is the least friction for a first-time broadcaster. Raise it (24 is the
    //   natural value) to break the killed -> request another -> back up in ninety seconds loop
    //   if abuse ever actually shows up. It is deliberately off by default because this app
    //   supplies no audience — a viewer cannot find a stream without a share link — so the payoff
    //   for abusing it is low, and the friction would fall entirely on legitimate users.
    //   PUBLISH_CODE_BATCH stamps an issuance cohort into new codes so /api/admin/revoke-batch can
    //   cut off everything minted before a bump. Bump it, don't reuse it.
    "PUBLISH_CODE_BATCH": "1",
    "PUBLISH_CODE_TTL_DAYS": "30",
    "PUBLISH_CODE_DELAY_HOURS": "0",
    "PUBLISH_CODE_POW_BITS": "18"
  },
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./simple",
    "binding": "ASSETS",
    // Serve /send.html and /view.html literally (no pretty-URL redirect to /send),
    // so the file names match the "two files" model + the download links, and the
    // relative view.html share link resolves without an extra 307 hop.
    "html_handling": "none"
  },
  "d1_databases": [
    {
      "binding": "DB",
      "database_name": "earthseed-db",
      "database_id": "b5065f2f-dc24-4ac2-94bd-b30432f0d288"
    }
  ],
  // Media-key salts. Holds the operator's global kill-switch salt ("salt:global")
  // and each live broadcast's rotatable salt ("salt:stream:<id>"). Salts are NOT
  // secret — they decode nothing without the #k= fragment key — so this is safe to
  // serve publicly. GLOBAL KILL SWITCH: rotate all stream keys at once with
  //   wrangler kv key put --binding=SALTS salt:global "<new-base64url>" --remote
  "kv_namespaces": [
    {
      "binding": "SALTS",
      "id": "f55a0dbce48d4e46b56a378520d418e3"
    }
  ],
  "durable_objects": {
    "bindings": [
      {
        "name": "CHAT_ROOMS",
        "class_name": "ChatRoom"
      }
    ]
  },
  "migrations": [
    {
      "tag": "v1",
      "new_sqlite_classes": ["ChatRoom"]
    }
  ],
  "routes": [
    {
      "pattern": "earthseed.live",
      "custom_domain": true
    },
    {
      "pattern": "www.earthseed.live",
      "custom_domain": true
    }
  ]
}
